.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Andrews&#x27;s CO2 isotherms showed a flat liquid-vapor plateau below a critical temperature and none above it. The van der Waals equation (~chemistrykit.thermo.systems.equations_of_state.VanDerWaals), built from CO2&#x27;s critical constants with from_critical_constants, reproduces the whole family: loops with three volume roots below T_c, an inflection at T_c, and single-valued isotherms above it, so gas can be turned into liquid continuously by going around the critical point.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_van_der_waals_continuity_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/plot_02_van_der_waals_continuity`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Andrews and van der Waals: continuity of the liquid and gas states</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/plot_02_van_der_waals_continuity`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Redlich and Kwong divided van der Waals&#x27;s attraction term by \sqrt{T}. This example draws CO2 P-V isotherms well above and just below the critical temperature from RedlichKwong, with VanDerWaals and IdealGas for comparison. Both real-gas equations are built from CO2&#x27;s critical temperature and pressure alone via from_critical_constants. Redlich-Kwong&#x27;s universal critical compressibility factor is 1/3, closer to real gases than van der Waals&#x27;s 3/8.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_03_redlich_kwong_thumb.png
